sections.s2_technical:
Many engineers reach for horizontal scaling reflexively: customers are unhappy, latency is up, so add more instances. This works beautifully when the constraint is CPU or memory in the stateless application layer, because each new instance adds genuine capacity. But in a multi-tenant system the bottleneck is frequently not in the stateless layer at all; it is in a resource that is shared across all instances and cannot be multiplied simply by cloning instances. In that case adding instances is not neutral — it is actively harmful, because each new instance is another client competing for the same scarce resource.

Consider the three classic shared bottlenecks. The first is a shared database. The database has a finite connection limit and finite I/O. If one large customer runs heavy queries, it consumes connections and I/O that all instances draw from. Adding instances increases the number of connections being opened, which can exhaust the database's connection limit faster and make things worse. The fix is a connection proxy like PgBouncer that multiplexes a bounded number of real database connections regardless of how many instances exist, plus per-tenant query concurrency limits so one customer cannot monopolise the database, plus read replicas to offload read-heavy tenants, and ultimately sharding so a large customer's data lives on its own database shard.

```java
// Adding instances multiplies DB connections → exhausts the shared limit faster
// Fix: a bounded shared proxy. 100 app instances → still only, say, 50 real DB connections.
DataSource ds = pgBouncerPool(maxRealConnections = 50); // instances share this cap
// Plus per-tenant query concurrency so one tenant cannot hold them all:
Semaphore tenantDbPermits = permitsFor(tenantId, maxConcurrentQueries = 5);
```

The second is a third-party API quota. If the bank integrates a payment gateway that allows one thousand calls per second across the whole account, that quota is shared by every instance. Adding instances does not raise the quota; it just means more instances racing to consume it, and a large customer can burn the entire quota, causing the provider to throttle everyone. The fix is a shared, distributed rate limiter that enforces per-tenant sub-quotas against the external API, so each tenant gets a fair slice of the thousand calls per second and no tenant can consume the whole budget.

```java
// Shared external quota (1000/s total) → per-tenant sub-quota enforced centrally (e.g. Redis)
boolean ok = distributedLimiter.tryAcquire("gateway", tenantId, perTenantQuota = 100); // fair slice
if (!ok) throw new QuotaExceededException(tenantId); // protect the shared external budget
```

The third is a single Kafka partition. Kafka orders messages within a partition, and a partition is consumed by exactly one consumer in a group. If a large customer's events all hash to one partition — because the partition key is the customer id — then all that customer's load lands on one partition and one consumer, and adding consumer instances does nothing because extra consumers for that group sit idle; a partition cannot be split across consumers. The fix is to increase the partition count and choose a partition key that spreads a large customer's load across many partitions (for example keying by a composite of customer and sub-entity), so the load parallelises, while preserving whatever ordering is genuinely required at a finer grain.

```java
// Hot partition: key = customerId → all BigCorp events on ONE partition, one consumer
// Fix: composite key spreads load across partitions while keeping per-account ordering
String key = customerId + ":" + accountId; // BigCorp's accounts now span many partitions
producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("transfers", key, event));
```

The senior-level nuance is diagnosis before action: you must identify which resource is actually saturated before choosing a remedy, because the remedy for a stateless-layer bottleneck (add instances) is the opposite of the remedy for a shared-resource bottleneck (bound and partition the shared resource). The telltale sign is that CPU and memory on the application instances are low while latency is high and errors are concentrated — that means the instances are waiting on something shared downstream. The general principle is that fairness and isolation must be enforced at the point of contention: per-tenant concurrency and quotas on the database, per-tenant sub-quotas on external APIs, and partition-key design plus partition count on messaging. Horizontal scaling of the stateless tier is a tool for one specific bottleneck, and using it against the wrong bottleneck wastes money and often deepens the outage.

